THOUGHT: NullAway complains that the constructor doesn’t guarantee non-null initialization of iterator1 and iterator2. They are lazily initialized in iterator(), but likely annotated or inferred as @NonNull. Easiest safe fix: initialize them eagerly in the constructor using the provided array and allowRemove, preserving existing lazy logic (the null check will just be false). I’ll modify the constructor accordingly and then run the NullAway build to confirm the error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/libgdx && sed -i '762,765c\    public ArrayIterable(Array<T> array, boolean allowRemove) {
      this.array = array;
      this.allowRemove = allowRemove;
      this.iterator1 = new ArrayIterator(array, allowRemove);
      this.iterator2 = new ArrayIterator(array, allowRemove);
    }' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/utils/Array.java && ./gradlew gdx:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -E "NullAway|error(s)?" new_build_output.log | tail -n 20
```

